# Per-session git worktrees. Several Claude sessions work this checkout at
# once; each takes its own worktree and branch so their edits cannot collide.
# Ignored because a worktree is a whole second checkout — one `git add -A`
# from any session would otherwise commit another session's entire tree.
.claude/worktrees/

# Screenshots of a local build. Only the live site writes to docs/screenshots,
# so a dev-server capture cannot overwrite a committed README image.
docs/screenshots/local/

# The accounts database and its WAL sidecars. `ACCOUNTS_DB` defaults to
# ./data/accounts.db, and running a dev server with ACCOUNTS_DEV_LOGIN=true
# creates it — so without this an accounts database (email, name, Google
# subject) is one `git add -A` away from being committed into history.
# `openStore` makes this directory itself, and no script can regenerate what
# is in it, so the whole of it is runtime state.
#
# The leading slash is load-bearing: a bare `data/` has no internal slash and
# so matches at any depth, which would silently swallow `src/data/` — the
# curated-data layer. Tracked files there are unaffected, but the next file
# added under it would be unstageable with nothing going red.
/data/

# Manim (scripts/manim/README.md): the virtualenv and manim's own output tree.
# The DELIVERABLE render is committed at docs/videos/ — this ignores the working
# copy manim writes beside the scene, which is the same bytes under a name that
# changes with the quality flag.
.venv-manim/
media/
# And the bytecode cache Python writes beside a scene the first time it is
# imported — a build artefact of running the tool, like `media/`.
__pycache__/

# Traffic snapshots. These are written on the HOST by
# shell_scripts/12_traffic_report.sh, into $DEPLOY_DIR/traffic-reports, and the
# app reads that directory directly — so the host is the source of truth and
# there is deliberately no committed copy to go stale against it. This entry is
# for the local ones: running the report against any nginx log, or the local
# dashboard window, writes summaries here.
#
# The leading slash matters for the reason /data/ carries one: a bare
# `traffic-reports/` has no internal slash and so would match at any depth.
/traffic-reports/
